# Target C — defect-charged train freedom and the exact locking-rank barrier

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Status:** proof-only structural theorem plus negative closure theorem; theorem state unchanged  
**Level I / C-II:** open

This note rederives the useful part of the old unreviewed ZTF/ZPL train-flex reports at
the current post-ZF scope and adds two facts not isolated there:

1. every bounded hole of the **full strict-rhombus complex** is paid injectively by
   bounded nonquadrilateral contact-face perimeter;
2. a pure Maxwell/rank attack on the resulting train freedom cannot close C-II.  The
   exact row-count surplus is determined by the positive-core vertices outside the
   rhombus complex and by `s_Z`.

The first fact gives a defect-sensitive neutral-space theorem.  The second identifies
precisely what a closing theorem must add: geometric rank loss of the external
contact/diameter rows on the rhombic shear space, not another row count.

No contact-isolated source point is removed or assigned a core velocity.

---

## 1. Full strict-rhombus complex

Work in the canonical post-ZF positive-contact core `G` on `m` vertices and `e`
contact edges.  Let

- `R` be the number of bounded strict-rhombus faces;
- `f` be the number of bounded nonquadrilateral contact faces;
- `Q=sum(deg F-4)` over those nonquadrilateral faces;
- `b` be the occurrence-counted outer contact-face length;
- `h` be the number of actual loaded diameter edges;
- `s_Z=e+h-(2m-2)>=0`.

Canonical face incidence gives

\[
2e=4R+(4f+Q)+b,
\tag{DF.1}
\]

and canonical Section 57 gives

\[
\boxed{b+Q=2(h-s_Z).}
\tag{DF.2}
\]

Let `mathcal R` be the union of all closed strict-rhombus faces, regarded as a finite
planar cell complex.  Write

- `V_R` for the number of core vertices incident to at least one strict rhombus;
- `E_R` for the number of contact edges incident to at least one strict rhombus;
- `c_R` for the number of connected components of `mathcal R` (vertex touching counts
  as connected for this topological count);
- `g_R` for the total number of bounded complementary components of `mathcal R`;
- `P_R` for the total physical boundary-edge occurrence count of `mathcal R`;
- `T:=P_R/2` for the total stopped-train count, summed over the edge-connected
  rhombus-face components.

The physical-boundary train theorem gives `P_R=2T`.  Face-edge incidence gives

\[
4R=2(E_R-P_R)+P_R,
\]

hence

\[
\boxed{E_R=2R+T.}
\tag{DF.3}

Planar Euler characteristic of the finite cell complex is

\[
V_R-E_R+R=c_R-g_R,
\]

so

\[
\boxed{V_R=R+T+c_R-g_R.}
\tag{DF.4}

These identities remain valid when different edge-connected rhombus-face components
touch at vertices; that is why `c_R` is the topological component count in (DF.4),
not the number of train components.

---

## 2. Hole boundary is paid by nonquadrilateral-face perimeter

Let `P_hole` be the total number of physical rhombus-boundary edge occurrences
bordering bounded complementary components of `mathcal R`.

### Theorem DF.1 — injective hole-boundary charge

\[
\boxed{P_{hole}\le4f+Q.}
\tag{DF.5}

### Proof

Take one physical rhombus-boundary edge `e` adjacent to a bounded complementary
component of `mathcal R`.  On one side of `e` lies a strict rhombus.  Because `e` lies
on a bounded hole, the other side is not the unbounded contact face.  It is therefore
incident to a bounded face of the full plane contact graph.

That other bounded face cannot be a strict rhombus: if it were, it would share the
full edge `e` with the first rhombus and hence belong to the same strict-rhombus union,
so `e` would not be physical boundary.  Since every bounded quadrilateral contact face
is a strict rhombus, the other face must be nonquadrilateral.

Thus each hole-boundary occurrence maps to the unique incidence of its edge with a
bounded nonquadrilateral face.  One face-edge incidence has only one opposite side, so
this map is injective over all rhombus components and all holes.  The total number of
such nonquadrilateral incidences is exactly `4f+Q`. `square`

This is stronger than merely using `P_hole<=P_R`: internal rhombic topology is paid by
one of the already existing defect currencies.

---

## 3. Every bounded rhombic hole has length at least five

### Lemma DF.2

Every bounded complementary component of `mathcal R` has physical boundary length at
least five.  Consequently

\[
\boxed{5g_R\le P_{hole}\le4f+Q.}
\tag{DF.6}

### Proof

A hole-boundary walk uses distinct boundary edges around that complementary component;
pinched vertices may repeat but do not create a repeated boundary edge.  Length one or
two is impossible in the simple straight-line contact graph.  Length three would be a
three-cycle of unit contacts, i.e. an exact unit equilateral triangle, excluded in the
C-II branch.

Suppose a bounded hole had length four.  A closed trail of four distinct edges in a
simple graph with no triangle is a four-cycle.  Its four straight sides are unit
contacts.  Four unit edge vectors summing to zero occur in opposite pairs, so the
simple quadrilateral is a unit rhombus.

The standard strict-rhombus coverage argument now applies: in a triangle-free unit
rhombus both diagonals exceed one in the required short-diagonal sense, and splitting
along the shorter diagonal shows that the two triangular halves have circumradius
strictly below one.  Hence every interior point of the rhombus is at distance less
than one from a boundary vertex.  No additional source point can lie inside it.
Therefore the four-cycle bounds an actual bounded quadrilateral contact face.  Every
such face is a strict rhombus, which would belong to `mathcal R` and share its boundary
edges with the surrounding rhombus complex.  It cannot be a complementary hole.

Thus every hole has at least five boundary occurrences.  Summing and applying
Theorem DF.1 gives (DF.6). `square`

In particular

\[
\boxed{g_R\le{4f+Q\over5}.}
\tag{DF.7}

---

## 4. Defect-sensitive train-angle flex dimension

For completeness we rederive the train-angle period argument rather than cite the old
unreviewed report.

Fix one edge-connected strict-rhombus face component `C`.  Give every stopped train
`tau` an oriented unit edge vector `u_tau` and an angular velocity `t_tau`.  On an
oriented rhombus edge `e` belonging to train `tau`, put

\[
\omega_t(e)=\sigma_e t_\tau J u_\tau,
\tag{DF.8}

where `sigma_e=+/-1` records the edge orientation.  Opposite sides of every rhombus
belong to the same train and cancel pairwise around the face, so `omega_t` has zero
period around every rhombus face.

If the component has `g` bounded holes, all remaining periods are represented by `g`
hole cycles.  Their vector periods give a linear map

\[
\mathcal P:\mathbb R^{T_C}\to(\mathbb R^2)^g
\]

of rank at most `2g`.  For `t in ker P`, the edge cochain has zero period on every
cycle and integrates to vertex velocities preserving every rhombus contact bar to
first order.  Hence the train-angle flex space has dimension at least

\[
T_C-2g.
\tag{DF.9}

Summing over edge-connected face components gives

\[
\boxed{
\dim\mathcal F_{train}
\ge T-2g_R
\ge T-{2\over5}(4f+Q).
}
\tag{DF.10}

This count is before adding arbitrary translations of disconnected bar components.
The constant train-angle vector on each edge-connected component is its Euclidean
rotation mode.

---

## 5. Exact outer-to-outer one-train shears

A stopped train endpoint on a bounded hole contributes a boundary edge occurrence to
`P_hole`.  Every train that is not outer-to-outer has at least one such endpoint.
Therefore, if `N_oo` is the number of trains whose two ends lie on unbounded-component
boundary,

\[
\boxed{
N_{oo}\ge T-P_{hole}\ge T-(4f+Q).
}
\tag{DF.11}

Each such train has an exact finite one-coordinate shear in the isolated rhombus
subframework.  To see this, rotate only its train vector `u_tau` by a small angle and
leave all other train vectors fixed.  Every rhombus still closes because opposite
sides use the same rotated vector.  A hole period contains only trains with an endpoint
on that hole boundary; an outer-to-outer train has none.  Thus all hole periods remain
exactly zero, and the modified edge vectors integrate globally on the component.

For sufficiently small angle all rhombi remain strict.  This statement concerns the
rhombus-edge subframework only.  Non-rhombus contact bars and diameter bars are exactly
the external locks that may obstruct the shear in the complete positive core.

---

## 6. The full rhombus bar framework has a Maxwell neutral-space floor

Let `R_R` be the planar bar-rigidity matrix using only the `E_R` rhombus edges and the
`V_R` rhombus-incident vertices.  Irrespective of special geometry,

\[
\operatorname{rank}R_R\le E_R.
\]

Hence

\[
\dim\ker R_R
\ge2V_R-E_R.
\]

Using (DF.3)--(DF.4),

\[
\boxed{
\dim\ker R_R
\ge T+2c_R-2g_R.
}
\tag{DF.12}

The train-period construction in Section 4 is a geometric subfamily of this general
Maxwell floor.

---

## 7. Exact barrier to a pure external-row-count closure

Let

\[
x:=m-V_R
\tag{DF.13}

be the number of positive-core vertices incident to no strict rhombus.  These are
**core** vertices, not contact-isolated source points.

The complete mixed active framework on the `m` core vertices has

\[
e+h=2m-2+s_Z
\tag{DF.14}

rows.  The number of rows not belonging to the rhombus-edge subframework is therefore

\[
N_{ext}:=e+h-E_R.
\tag{DF.15}

Extend a rhombus-subframework velocity by zero on the `x` outside core vertices.  If
all `N_ext` external rows also vanish, it is a flex of the full mixed active framework.
Canonical ZF says every such full flex is Euclidean.

Let `r_x` be the dimension of Euclidean infinitesimal motions that vanish on all `x`
outside vertices:

\[
r_x=
\begin{cases}
3,&x=0,\\
1,&x=1,\\
0,&x\ge2.
\end{cases}
\tag{DF.16}

(The `x=1` motions are rotations about the one fixed point.)  Therefore external rows
must have rank at least

\[
\dim\ker R_R-r_x.
\tag{DF.17}

If one uses only the guaranteed Maxwell floor (DF.12), the strongest possible
row-count demand is

\[
N_{ext}\ge(2V_R-E_R)-r_x.
\tag{DF.18}

But the difference between the **available** number of external rows and this demand is
an exact identity:

\[
\begin{aligned}
N_{ext}-[(2V_R-E_R)-r_x]
&=(e+h-E_R)-2V_R+E_R+r_x\\
&=2m-2+s_Z-2V_R+r_x\\
&=\boxed{2x-2+s_Z+r_x}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{DF.19}

Thus

\[
\boxed{
N_{ext}-[(2V_R-E_R)-r_x]
=
\begin{cases}
1+s_Z,&x=0,\\
1+s_Z,&x=1,\\
2x-2+s_Z,&x\ge2.
\end{cases}}
\tag{DF.20}

It is always nonnegative, and in fact at least `1+s_Z`.

### Consequence

A proof that uses only

```text
many rhombus/train flex dimensions
+ number of non-rhombus contact rows
+ number of diameter rows
```

cannot contradict ZF.  The exact post-ZF row ledger has enough external rows to kill
the universal Maxwell neutral space, with at least the one KKT/self-stress unit of
slack and additional slack from non-rhombic core vertices.

This is not merely a bad constant.  It is an algebraic barrier.

A closing theorem must prove one of the following stronger facts:

1. `rank` of the external rows **on the rhombic shear space** is strictly smaller than
   their row count by more than the margin in (DF.20);
2. the rhombus subframework has additional special-geometry flexes beyond the Maxwell
   floor, with a quantitatively sufficient excess;
3. a finite exact shear survives all external rows and yields a strict diameter
   decrease; or
4. the external locking rows needed by ordered trains are incompatible with the
   Woodall/attachment/corridor geometry.

The natural target is (1): a Hall/sparsity theorem for which external rows can see
which ordered train shears.

---

## 8. Type-II scale

Canonical Type II has

\[
A_4/D^2\to\pi/4.
\]

For every train, its total transverse rhombus-area span is at most `D`, and every
rhombus belongs to two trains.  Hence

\[
2A_4\le TD,
\]

so

\[
\boxed{T\ge(\pi/2-o(1))D.}
\tag{DF.21}

Thus the neutral/locking problem is genuinely linear scale.  Equations (DF.7),
(DF.10), and (DF.11) show exactly how bounded-face defect can erode that train freedom;
(DF.20) shows why scalar row count alone still cannot finish the proof.

If a later endpoint theorem forces `4f+Q=o(D)`, then (DF.10) gives

\[
\dim\mathcal F_{train}\ge(\pi/2-o(1))D,
\]

and (DF.11) gives the same leading lower bound on exact outer-to-outer one-train
shears.  At that point essentially the entire linear train family must be locked by
the boundary/diameter system, making external-row **geometry** rather than internal
rhombus topology the sole obstruction.

---

## 9. Scope and hostile audit

- `x=m-V_R` counts positive-core vertices only.  Contact-isolated source points never
  enter the mixed framework or the Maxwell calculation.
- The hole charge is by physical edge occurrence and is injective.  It does not count
  one nonquadrilateral face incidence twice.
- A four-edge hole is excluded only after proving it would be an actual empty
  quadrilateral contact face and hence a strict rhombus in the same complex.
- The train-angle theorem is first-order.  Only the outer-to-outer coordinate shears in
  Section 5 are asserted as exact finite rhombus-subframework deformations.
- No claim is made that those shears preserve non-rhombus contact bars or diameter
  bars; those are the locking rows under study.
- Equation (DF.20) deliberately records a failed closure route.  It must not be read as
  evidence that the external rows actually attain full possible rank on train shears.
  Their geometric rank is the live question.
- The theorem-state manifest is unchanged.

